		{
			"id": "ejP83RdqNSTYuCNwV",
			"description": "Ork Scrap Flak",
			"local_notes": "Weak Points 8: when a hit on this armour fails to penetrate, roll 3d; on 8 or less it found a gap and DR is halved against it.\n\nA patchwork of scavenged plate, tin and leather straps; no two suits look alike, and a GM may reasonably rule that a given salvaged pauldron or plate has already been dented, holed or is simply missing on any individual figure.\n\nLore: The armour of Gretchin, unproven Boyz, and any Ork too poor or too new to have earned better kit: whatever scrap plate, riveted tin and leather a Boy can scavenge or is issued by a Nob who doesn't want him dying quite that easily. Codex: Orks background and army art both depict most rank-and-file Boyz in exactly this kind of light, mismatched protection rather than a proper suit.\n\nDesign: No framework anchor names this item directly, so it is built as a lighter cousin of the 'Eavy Armour anchor below rather than an independent figure: roughly half the anchor's DR 45, giving DR 20, per the same \"lighter, gappier version of the heavier pattern\" logic this project uses for the Imperium's own Scout Armour relative to full power armour. Exact dice distribution (updated to match the Ranged Weapons file's own corrected Slugga and Shoota dice): a Slugga (7d pi+) penetrates it 80.8% of the time and a Shoota (9d pi+) 98.5% of the time - genuinely poor protection against Ork small arms themselves, let alone Imperial weapons, matching its place as the worst armour in either army's roster. Weak Points 8, worse (gappier) than the 'Eavy Armour's own already-poor 7, per the framework's Ork rule that Ork armour \"is thick scrap plate with many gaps,\" taken to its logical extreme for the cheapest tier of that armour. Coverage is deliberately partial - arms only, no head or hand protection - matching the scavenged, mismatched look the lore describes.",
