		{
			"id": "eiF-DLeHngC7Ht0Pb",
			"description": "Kustom Force Field",
			"local_notes": "A regenerating shield per framework.md's personal-force-field rules, but built and sized for a mob rather than a single wearer: every Ork (and only Orks and their vehicles - the field does not recognise or protect anything it doesn't believe is \"one of da boyz\") within 5 yards of the Mek carrying the projector is covered against all incoming damage, not just the Mek himself. Shield Points: 250. Delay: 4 seconds after last taking damage before it starts recharging. Recharge: 15 SP/second once it starts. Collapse (SP hits 0): needs double the normal delay (8 seconds) before it starts recharging again, as any regenerating shield. Track current SP with the item's Uses counter in GCS, shared across every Ork it is currently covering. Erratic: this is a Mek's kustom bodge, not a refined Imperial pattern - each time the field would absorb an attack, roll 1d first; on a 1, the field simply fails to activate for that hit (\"it wasn't lookin'\"), and the attack proceeds against the wearer's armour and DR as if the field weren't there at all, with no SP lost either way.\n\nLore: A Big Mek's bubble-shield generator, projecting a crackling energy dome over himself and every Ork fighting nearby; Codex: Orks background describes it as capable of shrugging off small-arms fire and even heavier weapons across an entire mob at once, at the cost of being wildly inconsistent - the same background material notes that Kustom Force Fields have an infamous habit of interfering with teleporters and deep-strike landings near them, a detail left here as colour for a GM running such a scene rather than a new mechanic this project would need to invent whole-cloth.\n\nDesign: Built on the framework's regenerating-shield table (Refractor 40 SP, Rosarius 60, Conversion 80, Iron Halo 120, Storm Shield 160, all at 2-3 second delays and 10-20 SP/second recharge) rather than a flat DR bonus, as the framework requires for personal force fields. A KFF is built above every entry on that table (250 SP) since it covers an entire mob's incoming fire rather than one wearer, the same logic that makes a Storm Shield's 160 the ceiling for a single-target field; it is a mob-scale field the framework table does not list, and shares one SP pool among every Ork it covers. The erratic quality is a flat 1-in-6 chance, rolled once per attack (a full-auto burst counts as one attack, so one 1d roll covers the whole burst), that the field simply does not answer: not a reduced SP pool or slower recharge, either of which would only make it a smaller version of an Imperial field rather than a genuinely unreliable one. Delay (4 s) and Collapse (8 s) are double the Storm Shield's 2 s delay, reflecting a hand-built Mek generator's slower recovery compared to Imperial-manufactured fields. TL 11 like the Imperial fields it copies, with Mek Instinct offsetting the TL penalty for the Meks who build it. Weight 60 lb and a five-figure cost reflect a major, backpack-scale piece of Mek engineering, above any single-wearer field in this project's Imperium armour file.",
			"tags": [
				"Body Armor",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "15000",
			"base_weight": "60 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eettD0KU40vkIoYHP",
			"description": "Cybork Body",
			"local_notes": "A worn-gear stand-in only; see design for why this project's own conventions say a Cybork Body is better built as a trait.\n\nLore: Orks who lose limbs or organs to the fortunes of war and survive their Painboy's attention often come back with crude bionic replacements - a metal arm, a mechanical eye, a riveted plate over a healed skull wound. Codex: Orks background treats \"going cybork\" as a badge of survival as much as a practical fix, and a sufficiently rebuilt Ork Nob or Warboss may be more machine than meat.\n\nDesign: Per framework.md's own Adeptus Mechanicus precedent (\"Model augmetics as traits... one per implant, so a character sheet lists what has been replaced\"), a Cybork Body is genuinely better built as a set of GURPS cybernetic advantage traits (an Injury Tolerance/DR combination and whatever limb-replacement or Payload traits fit a given Ork's actual injuries) than as a single piece of equipment, exactly the same reasoning the framework already applies to Tech-Priest and Skitarii augmetics. This entry exists as a simple, optional at-the-table stand-in for a GM who doesn't want to build a bespoke trait package on the spot: a flat, modest DR overlay (torso/arm/leg 5, stacking with worn armour) representing scattered plates and reinforced bone rather than a coherent suit, priced and weighted as a rough abstraction, not a considered anchor figure. A campaign that expects to field named, recurring cyborked Orks (a Warboss, a veteran Nob) should build them the trait-file way instead, per the framework's own stated preference.",
