		{
			"id": "eYou4D7fpYwMWVyMg",
			"description": "Gitfinda",
			"local_notes": "A crude optical sight, fitted to a shoota-family weapon (Shoota, Kustom Shoota, Big Shoota, Deffgun or a Kombi-Weapon's shoota barrel): grants +1 to that weapon's listed Acc. It does nothing for a pistol, rokkit tube or energy weapon - there's no housing on this project's Ork pistols and launchers for a Mek to bolt one onto.\n\nLore: A salvaged or Mek-built optical sight bolted onto a shoota, letting an Ork actually aim rather than simply point and spray; Codex: Orks background treats gitfindas as an uncommon but real upgrade some Boyz and Nobz scrounge or are issued, distinct from the plain iron sights every other Ork gun makes do with.\n\nDesign: No framework anchor covers accessories, so this is built the same way the Imperium's own scopes and sights work in GURPS terms - a flat +1 Acc bonus to the host weapon, the smallest meaningful step on the Acc scale, reflecting a genuinely crude optic rather than a proper scoped sight (which would call for +2 or more, per the framework's own \"sniper and scoped weapons +1 to +3\" guidance - a gitfinda earns the bottom of that range, not the top). Restricted to shoota-family long arms since every other Ork ranged weapon in this file either has no obvious mounting rail (pistols, the rokkit tubes) or is already built around its own targeting quirk (the Zzap Gun's crackling charge, the Shokk Attack Gun's warp field) that a bolt-on sight wouldn't meaningfully improve. Handling: 2 lb, a small accessory; TL6 and LC0 match the rest of this file's Ork-tech entries.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks",
				"Gear"
			],
			"tech_level": "6",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "150",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eidrcNYxgk9EZWETb",
			"description": "Ammo Runt",
			"local_notes": "Represents a Gretchin attached to a Boy or Nob specifically to lug spare ammunition, not an inanimate item - the weight above is 0 because the runt, not the gunner, carries the load. Mechanically: any one weapon the gunner carries with a shots line ending in a reload time in parentheses (e.g. \"30(5)\") treats that reload as one full second faster, to a minimum of 1 second, since the runt has the next drum or belt ready to hand rather than making the gunner dig for it himself. A runt this close to the fighting is exactly as fragile as the framework's Gretchin template (ST 8, HP 8, no armour worth the name) and will flee, hide, or simply vanish at the first serious sign of danger, per Gretchin cowardice - a GM should treat losing an ammo runt mid-fight as a real and recurring risk, not a one-time purchase that then always works.\n\nLore: Gretchin are routinely pressed into service as ammunition bearers, loaders and general dogsbodies for Boyz and Nobz who can't be bothered carrying their own spare drums; Codex: Orks background treats this as one of the more mundane, constant uses Orks have for their Gretchin underlings, alongside minding squigs and other equally thankless jobs.\n\nDesign: Not a piece of equipment in the normal sense (a hired or press-ganged Gretchin is a creature, not gear), but included in the same spirit as the Imperium's generic support-gear entries (the Heavy Weapon Tripod, the Assault Cannon Ammunition Hopper) that give a logistics detail a concrete cost and effect rather than leaving it purely narrative. Built as a small, capped reload-time discount rather than an unlimited-ammo effect, since a second pair of hands passing you drums faster is a real but modest benefit, not a reason to remove reload time from the game, and the note is explicit that the runt himself is a liability, not a fire-and-forget purchase, true to a Gretchin's place in Ork background as expendable, unreliable and prone to running.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks",
				"Gear"
			],
			"tech_level": "4",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "30",
			"base_weight": "0 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eZ0Zoj3lA1bnsWFRe",
			"description": "Stikkbomb Belt",
			"local_notes": "A webbing belt or bandolier holding 6 Stikkbombs (Frag), carried ready to throw.\n\nLore: The standard way a Boy carries his stikkbombs into a fight - a bandolier or belt of tin-can bombs slung across the chest, as ubiquitous in Ork army art as the choppa and slugga themselves.\n\nDesign: A pure carrying-capacity item, built the same way this project's Imperium files handle spare-magazine and ammunition entries (e.g. the Autogun Magazine, spare): it exists to give a Boy's grenade loadout a weight and cost rather than assuming an unlimited supply, with the actual Stikkbomb (Frag) entry above carrying the weapon's damage and design derivation.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"tech_level": "4",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "30",
			"base_weight": "6 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		}
	]
